            <div xml:id="d920">

                <pb facs="1028" n="884" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg884"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">821.7961/16: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Colombia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bogotá</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-02-27T16:00:00-05:00">February 27, 1929—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:20 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>28. My telegram number 27, February 19, 7 p.m. Monday last, Minister for Foreign
                    Affairs showed me Olaya’s latest telegram saying he had signed note and had sent
                    copies regulations by air mail. I asked him what steps Pan American should take
                    in order to begin service. He replied of course he did not know exactly what
                    regulations stipulated but that all he now wanted was formal notice through the
                    Legation that Pan American desired to initiate service; he thought it would be
                    well to include usual data about machines and names of pilots.</p>
                <p>However, Doctor Uribe believes this to be the reciprocal procedure stipulated in
                    the regulations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
